            {
                "entry_id": "df93f6fd-c311-32e8-900c-b75f7ac64a55",
                "word": "guan",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "a6d70322-0c7b-4e50-8fe2-2f811b01544e",
                        "entry_id": "df93f6fd-c311-32e8-900c-b75f7ac64a55",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "guans"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "09178bef-a85d-3229-a2d3-3d1ff497eb98",
                        "entry_id": "df93f6fd-c311-32e8-900c-b75f7ac64a55",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any (member) of several species of birds in the genera Aburria, Chamaepetes, Oreophasis, Penelope, Penelopina, and Pipile, of the family Cracidae, limited to the Americas."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a4191ef1-17ac-4fee-b8de-01ce32bba32d",
                                "sense_id": "09178bef-a85d-3229-a2d3-3d1ff497eb98",
                                "text": "The 'true' (Penelope) guans also occur at a range of altitudes but like most species of cracids, are mostly restricted to forest, both montane and lowland. Piping-guans (Pipile) are primarily lowland species, whereas numerous monospecific (single-species genus) taxa of guans (i.e. Aburria, Penelopina, Oreophasis) are restricted to montane environments."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "484e7d92-9d12-4b3d-b3fb-9c5a7b43a694",
                                "sense_id": "09178bef-a85d-3229-a2d3-3d1ff497eb98",
                                "text": "Most guans move among only the most rewarding trees, those having the largest fruit crops and/or the greatest reward per fruit. Nevertheless, solitary individuals and some guan groups - as well as the first-mentioned guans, once they deplete the fruit crops in the most popular trees - are stuck with the less rewarding trees."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "487ebcc4-663e-4c08-9796-6156114fd570",
                                "sense_id": "09178bef-a85d-3229-a2d3-3d1ff497eb98",
                                "text": "The Crested Guan is a large, brown, turkeylike bird most frequently seen peering down from the treetops as it searches for fruit and leaves that make up its diet. As its name implies, this bird has a feathered crest that is frequently raised as it relates to nearby guans or to potential danger."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
